#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/id #uuid "6a492cbf-d378-5193-baf2-c88a2bea63fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21774" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x)$.\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156985 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "9a26313d-4b93-5d82-98c1-dbecb0bead82" :question/math-academy-id "e-21775" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$ $AAAAA$." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u$, we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(ue^{5x})^{′} - 5(ue^{5x}) &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} + 5ue^{5x} - 5ue^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} + 5ue^{5x} - 5ue^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′} &= e^{-5x}\\cos 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= e^{-5x}\\cos 2x \\\\\n∫u^{′}dx &= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx \\\\\nu &= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\ny_p = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= u(x)e^{5x} \\\\\n&= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx \\cdot e^{5x} \\\\\n&= e^{5x}∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156986 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "bafa3bf1-a867-5259-a6da-226195b45541" :question/math-academy-id "e-21777" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$ $AAAAA$." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u$, we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(ue^{3x})^{′} - 3(ue^{3x}) &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} - 3ue^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} - 3ue^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′} &= 12e^{-3x}\\sinh 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= 12e^{-3x}\\sinh 3x \\\\\n&= 12e^{-3x}(\\frac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\\n&= 6e^{-3x}(e^{3x} - e^{-3x}) \\\\\n&= 6 - 6e^{-6x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= 6 - 6e^{-6x} \\\\\n∫u^{′}dx &= ∫6 - 6e^{-6x}dx \\\\\nu &= 6x + e^{-6x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\ny_p = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= u(x)e^{3x} \\\\\n&= 6xe^{3x} + e^{-3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "841b977e-80d4-5643-a50c-d00e3917bb74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155374} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a79cea-33fe-5602-938c-728bdaa6b324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155375} {:answer/id #uuid "efb7d0f1-d310-52a8-97e8-7ffb93cbb32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155376} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff8e560-14df-565d-8e02-7d9a1f3cc6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{− 4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155377} {:answer/id #uuid "30ecd628-e3df-5f9c-8b57-329a0da8b0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x)e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155378}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3511e1-d6c3-5d05-9adf-3ff8bc8cfca0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e22471a-3a2e-5481-a210-ff1bf839c5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)e^{− 6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155380} {:answer/id #uuid "1a101238-ee20-58a8-a2a3-c031acbb7d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(6)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155381} {:answer